00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  this was phil newby before his illness  an active father of two young girls  but aged 43 everything changed you got  diagnosed in may didn't you  behind the smile on this family holiday  he was coming to terms with a diagnosis  of  motor neurone disease  five years on the disease had taken its  toll  and with the support of his family phil  had made a decision to fight in the  courts  for the right to an assisted death he  spoke then of his biggest fear  it's being locked in without any  capacity to  to move only only my eyes and perhaps  eyelids might work  and that i live in fear of that two  years have now passed and phil's lungs  are failing  this is how he spends two hours each day  his legal battle was rejected his only  hope now  a new attempt in parliament to change  the law  this situation is a nightmare and my end  is  most likely to be lingering and  unpleasant  full of anguish and anxiety and physical  suffering  and i don't see why i should have to go  through that in the house of lords  the first step to introduce bill to  enable adults who are terminally ill to  be provided at their request  with specified assistance to end their  own life this should  pave the way for the first debate on  assisted dying in parliament  since 2015. last time it was  overwhelmingly rejected  but campaigners sense a shift more  doctors now support  assisted dying and more countries are  legalizing it  they think this time mps could vote  in favor but there are groups including  many disability rights campaigners  who fear the consequences of a change in  the law  there are concerns about how there may  be pressures towards  going down the route of assisted suicide  instead of  having good support such as palliative  care or good support just to live a life  you want to have  for phil newby time is running out if  law change happens  it may be too late for him becky johnson  sky news

